> The precise problem and its feasibility condition

We seek a spanning directed out-arborescence rooted at r: r has no incoming
selected arc, every other vertex has exactly one, and every vertex is reachable
from r. Those conditions force exactly V−1 arcs and exclude directed cycles.
This is a prescribed-root spanning problem, not an unconstrained minimum
branching forest, where omitting positive-cost arcs could give a cheaper empty
answer. It is also different from choosing an undirected MST.

An arborescence exists exactly when every original vertex is reachable from r.
Necessity is immediate. For sufficiency, a rooted search can choose each newly
reached vertex's first incoming discovery arc. The algorithm need not run that
search separately: a missing incoming choice, possibly after contracting a
closed cycle, detects nonexistence. Checking only the initial incoming choices
is insufficient.

> Why normalisation and contraction preserve the optimum

Let b(v) be the cheapest eligible incoming cost for each non-root vertex and
b(r)=0. Every feasible tree chooses one incoming arc for each non-root vertex,
so subtracting b(v) from every arc entering v changes every feasible objective
by the same baseline:

$$
B=\sum_{v\ne r}b(v),\qquad
w(T)=B+\sum_{(u,v)\in T}\bigl(w(u,v)-b(v)\bigr).
$$
\alttext(The cost of any feasible tree is the sum of all chosen minimum incoming baselines plus the sum of its reduced arc costs.)

Every eligible reduced cost is nonnegative, and the chosen incoming arcs have
reduced cost zero. If they are acyclic, following predecessors must end at r:
every other vertex has a predecessor, while r has none. They already form an
optimum arborescence with reduced cost zero.

If they contain a zero-cost cycle C, an optimum can be chosen with just one
external arc entering C. To see this, take a root-to-C path in an optimum and
keep its first entry. Replace the other incoming choices in C with the
zero-cost cycle arcs, deleting the cycle arc entering that first entry vertex.
All of C becomes reachable along a directed path, outside reachability remains,
and no reduced cost increases. Contracting C therefore preserves an optimum.
Different entry arcs retain their original identities and reduced costs;
parallel entries into a contracted component must remain distinct.

The implementation contracts all disjoint selected cycles in a round. Edges
that become internal remain stored as self loops but are ignored in future
incoming choices. On expansion, the chosen original entering arc identifies
exactly which previous incoming arc to replace. Current-component maps for
original vertices support repeated expansion through nested cycles.
Finite negative original weights are valid: the same normalisation applies,
and a tree cannot traverse a negative cycle repeatedly.

> Nested cycles need more than one contraction

Use arcs 0→1 of cost 5, 0→3 of cost 10, the zero-cost pair 1↔2, and
arcs 2→3 and 3→1 of cost 1. Initially {1,2} is a selected cycle and
vertex 3 chooses 2→3. After contraction, the component {1,2} and vertex 3
select each other. A second contraction is required before a root entry wins.
The native expansion recovers 0→1, 1→2 and 2→3 with total cost 6.

The diagram labels each group with its actual current vertex id and original
members. Display order follows the smallest original member for readability;
the kernel's cycle-first component ids are retained in the labels. Every arrow
comes from the actual next-component map.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/chu_liu_nested.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<dm::algorithms::WeightedEdge> edges{{0,1,5},{0,3,10},{1,2,0},{2,1,0},{2,3,1},{3,1,1}};
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::chu_liu_edmonds_trace(4,edges,0);
  if(t.phases.size()!=3 || t.tree.result.total_weight!=6)return 1;
  std::print("| actual phase | current vertices | selected cycles | baseline added | cumulative reduced cost |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(60,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.width=3.4;node.height=1.6;node.font_size=20;
  for(std::size_t p=0;p<t.phases.size();++p) {
    const auto& phase=t.phases[p];
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",p,phase.vertex_count,phase.cycles.size(),phase.added_cost,phase.cumulative_cost);
    std::vector<std::vector<std::size_t>> members(phase.vertex_count);
    for(std::size_t v=0;v<phase.original_to_vertex.size();++v)members[phase.original_to_vertex[v]].push_back(v);
    std::vector<std::size_t> order(phase.vertex_count);std::iota(order.begin(),order.end(),0);
    std::sort(order.begin(),order.end(),[&](auto a,auto b){return members[a].front()<members[b].front();});
    for(std::size_t j=0;j<order.size();++j) {
      const auto v=order[j];bool in_cycle=false;
      for(const auto& cycle:phase.cycles)if(std::ranges::find(cycle,v)!=cycle.end())in_cycle=true;
      node.fill=v==phase.root?dm::graphics::RGB{220,252,231}:(in_cycle?dm::graphics::RGB{254,240,138}:dm::graphics::RGB{219,234,254});
      diagram.node(std::format("p{}v{}",p,v),{15.0*j/(order.size()-1),-5.0*p},std::format("current id {}\n{}",v,members[v]),node);
    }
  }
  for(std::size_t p=0;p+1<t.phases.size();++p)for(std::size_t v=0;v<t.phases[p].vertex_count;++v)
    diagram.edge(std::format("p{}v{}",p,v),std::format("p{}v{}",p+1,t.phases[p].component[v]));
  diagram.text({7.5,2.6},"Actual partitions: {1,2}, then {1,2,3}",22)
      .text({7.5,-12.6},std::format("Recovered original parents {}; total cost {}",t.tree.parent,t.tree.result.total_weight),20)
      .save_svg("build/chu_liu_nested.svg");
  std::print("\nRecovered parents: {}. Compact saved vertex entries: {}. Actual expansion assignments: {}.\n",t.tree.parent,t.work.saved_vertex_entries,t.work.expansion_replacements);
}
