\caption(Two nested contractions reduce four vertices to three and then two before expansion recovers the original tree.)
\alttext(Three rows show the original four singleton vertices, then groups {0}, {1,2}, {3}, then {0}, {1,2,3}. Root groups are green; currently cycling groups are gold. Arrows follow native component maps.)

Cycle lists in the API follow predecessor links, which is the reverse of
traversing their selected directed arcs. Group membership and selected edge
identities specify the cycle without relying on an ambiguous drawing order.
The routine now uses iterative cycle detection that follows each previously
unprocessed non-root vertex once per successful round, rather than repeatedly
walking a long predecessor chain.

> Checking a family with a known optimum and scan count

The native rooted_cycle_edges(k) fixture has root 0, cycle vertices 1…k,
a unit-cost directed cycle, and root-entry cost 5 to every cycle vertex.
All k initial choices form one cycle. One root entry is necessary, and every
other chosen arc costs at least 1, so OPT≥5+(k−1)=k+4. Keeping one root
entry and deleting the cycle arc into its target attains that lower bound.

This family takes two incoming-choice rounds and one contraction. Every round
retains all 2k input arcs, including mapped self loops. Thus actual incoming
scans inspect 4k arcs and reweighting inspects 2k, for a named total of 6k.
These counts exclude validation, parent walks, copying, expansion and allocation;
they are not elapsed time or a worst-case complexity measurement.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/chu_liu_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> sizes,actual,prediction;
  std::print("| cycle k | input arcs | rounds | incoming scans | reweight visits | named total | tree cost | known OPT |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const std::size_t k:{2,3,4,8,16,32,64,128}) {
    const auto edges=dm::algorithms::rooted_cycle_edges(k);const auto t=dm::algorithms::chu_liu_edmonds_trace(k+1,edges,0);
    const auto total=t.work.incoming_edge_visits+t.work.reweight_edge_visits;
    if(t.work.phases!=2 || total!=6*k || t.tree.result.total_weight!=k+4)return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",k,edges.size(),t.work.phases,t.work.incoming_edge_visits,t.work.reweight_edge_visits,total,t.tree.result.total_weight,k+4);
    sizes.push_back(k);actual.push_back(total);prediction.push_back(6*k);
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(sizes,actual,"actual",{37,99,235},1.5).points(sizes,prediction,"6k",{234,88,12},5)
      .legend({"actual named edge visits","two-round prediction 6k"},{{37,99,235},{234,88,12}})
      .title("One cycle: two scans and one reweighting pass")
      .x_label("cycle vertices k").y_label("named edge inspections")
      .x_limits(0,135).y_limits(0,850).save_svg("build/chu_liu_work.svg");
}
