// result_end
\caption(Exact caps pass, lower caps fail, ignored malformed input rejects, and a wide intermediate remains distinct from final-total overflow.)

A root is required even for an edgeless graph; zero vertices or an out-of-range
root throw std::invalid_argument. A singleton root has a valid zero-cost tree.
Vertex ids stay size_t throughout, and traversal/expansion is iterative. Calls
borrow input without mutation and return owned values. long double's extra
range and precision are platform-dependent, and nonfinite working arithmetic
still throws. No last-bit reproducibility across floating implementations is
promised.

> Verification and limits

The native tests independently enumerate one incoming choice per non-root
vertex and check rooted reachability. All 2,187 three-vertex signed ternary
graph/root combinations and 16,384 four-vertex presence/root combinations agree
with that brute-force optimum. Another 1,000 seeded signed multigraphs check
parallel identities, roots and impossible cases. Every actual phase is checked
for its cheapest incoming choices, reduced costs, cycle membership, component
maps, baseline and named work. The recovered original edges are verified to
form a tree with the returned cost.

Nested and simultaneous cycles, negative costs, exact and one-below caps,
malformed ignored arcs, owned copies, huge size_t domains, wide intermediate
costs, positive/negative final overflow and a 100,000-vertex chain are covered.
All fourteen new cases pass AddressSanitizer and UBSan, alongside the existing
advanced-graph regressions in the native runner. The controlled 6k plot is a
check of one two-round family, not evidence that all instances require only
two rounds or linear work.

> References

- Y.-J. Chu and T.-H. Liu (1965), *On the shortest arborescence of a directed
  graph*, Scientia Sinica 14(10), 1396–1400.
  https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~15850/handouts/chu-liu_1965.pdf
- Jack Edmonds (1967), *Optimum Branchings*, Journal of Research of the National
  Bureau of Standards, Section B, 71B(4), 233–240.
  https://doi.org/10.6028/jres.071B.032
